{"id":"n-20261010-vf65qk","category":"gaming","display_title":"The Peculiar Persona Demon","display_title_he":"השד המוזר בסדרה פרסונה","short_summary":"An exploration of Mara, a notorious phallic demon appearing across Atlus's popular Persona role-playing games.","english_level":"C1","words":473,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"Polygon","domain":"polygon.com","url":"https://www.polygon.com/persona-atlus-scariest-mara","title":"Persona’s Horrifying Dong Monster Will Haunt My Nightmares Forever","published_at":"2026-10-10T10:00:15.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-10T10:00:15.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-10T12:51:18.925Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":9,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["The acclaimed Persona series of role-playing games, developed by Atlus, incorporates a vast array of mythological and folkloric creatures drawn from traditions around the world. Some of these entities are formidable warriors like Odin from Norse mythology or the Hindu goddess Lakshmi, while others are cute and charming characters like Alice from Alice in Wonderland or the spooky snowman Jack Frost. They function similarly to Pokémon, allowing the protagonist to equip various entities possessing distinct skills, strengths, and weaknesses. However, one specific creature stands apart from the rest, requiring a censored version due to its extreme design. This figure is named Mara, and his presence has elicited astonishment, confusion, and profound discomfort among players for years.","Visually, Mara is depicted as a giant, green phallic entity featuring four arms, multiple tentacles, and a gaping mouth, all while riding a golden chariot. In Buddhist tradition, Mara represents a demon associated with temptation, lust, and earthly pleasures. The Sanskrit origin of the name translates directly to \"the Destroyer\" or \"the Evil One.\" Furthermore, the character serves as a clever visual pun, because the word \"mara\" functions as Japanese slang for a penis, although this specific anatomical depiction does not appear anywhere else in pop culture.","Players encounter this unconventional character across multiple titles, including most Shin Megami Tensei games, Persona 3, Persona 4, and Persona 5, alongside spin-offs like Persona Q and Persona 5 Strikers. The creature achieves peak prominence in Persona 5 as the \"Torn King of Desire,\" serving as a boss during the game's very first dungeon. During this particular combat encounter, the boss will occasionally waste a turn simply staring at the attractive blonde party member, Ann Takamaki. In later stages of the game, the entity appears sporadically as a regular enemy renamed the \"Throbbing King of Desire.\"","Despite its startling appearance, Mara generally functions as an optional and exceptionally powerful summon rather than an unavoidable adversary in most titles. Developers originally slated the tubular terror to appear in the 2024 release Metaphor ReFantazio, but ultimately removed the character from the final version, presumably to secure a T for Teen rating from the ESRB. Even so, the creature maintains a dedicated following, famously claiming the number-one spot in an official popularity poll conducted by Atlus in 2017.","Encountering this unexpected design often startles players who are entirely unprepared for such explicit imagery in a mainstream role-playing game. While sex and horror frequently pair together in narrative media from vampire tales to slasher movies, the Persona series are not horror games. Encountering Mara for the first time feels jarring and unusual, akin to witnessing someone expose themselves on public transit or receiving an unsolicited image. Reviewers note that if there is one element that gives pause about recommending these games to friends beyond the massive runtimes or hot springs scenes, it is the distinct chance of running into Mara."],"facts":[{"fact":"Atlus's Persona series incorporates various mythological creatures functioning like Pokémon.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"One notable creature in the series is Mara, a green phallic demon with four arms, tentacles, and a mouth, riding a golden chariot.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Mara is a Buddhist demon associated with temptation, lust, and earthly pleasures, with the Sanskrit name meaning 'the Destroyer' or 'the Evil One'.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The name is also a visual pun, as 'mara' is Japanese slang for penis.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Mara has appeared in Shin Megami Tensei games, Persona 3, 4, 5, and spin-offs, notably serving as the 'Torn King of Desire' boss in Persona 5.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"During the Persona 5 boss fight, Mara occasionally wastes a turn staring at Ann Takamaki.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Mara was meant to appear in 2024’s Metaphor ReFantazio but was cut from the final version.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Mara won an official 2017 Atlus poll for popularity among demons.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"acclaimed","lemma":"acclaimed","pos":"adj","he":"מהולל, זוכה שבחים","name":false},{"text":"Persona","lemma":"Persona","pos":"noun","he":"פרסונה","name":true},{"text":"series","lemma":"series","pos":"noun","he":"סדרה","name":false},{"text":"role-playing","lemma":"role-playing","pos":"adj","he":"תפקידים (משחקי תפקידים)","name":false},{"text":"games","lemma":"game","pos":"noun","he":"משחקים","name":false},{"text":"developed","lemma":"develop","pos":"verb","he":"פותח","name":false},{"text":"Atlus","lemma":"Atlus","pos":"noun","he":"אטלס","name":true},{"text":"incorporates","lemma":"incorporate","pos":"verb","he":"משלב","name":false},{"text":"vast","lemma":"vast","pos":"adj","he":"עצום","name":false},{"text":"array","lemma":"array","pos":"noun","he":"מערך","name":false},{"text":"mythological","lemma":"mythological","pos":"adj","he":"מיתולוגי","name":false},{"text":"folkloric","lemma":"folkloric","pos":"adj","he":"פולקלוריסטי","name":false},{"text":"creatures","lemma":"creature","pos"